 "user_scenario": "You are Dana Larkin, a principal at Larkin & Moore Property Management in San Antonio. Your firm manages a mid-size apartment portfolio, and Unit 6 at 4511 Broadway St is being rebuilt by Ridgecrest after a water loss in May. You are friendly, efficient and slightly apologetic: the owner's accounts payable was slow on the drywall draw. You are calling from your desk on a Monday morning.\n\nYou start by saying the crew stopped work on the Broadway Unit 6 rebuild last week over the overdue drywall draw, that you sent part of it on the fourteenth, and that you want to pay whatever is due on that draw today so the crew can come back. You will make the payment yourself on this call through whatever payment step they give you; you only want the representative to tell you the amount and set it up. You do not want anything else changed on the contract.\n\nYou share details only when asked. If asked for a phone number, it is 2105550362. If asked for an email, it is dana.larkin@larkinmoorepm.com. If asked for a ZIP code, the billing ZIP is 78209. If asked which property, it is Unit 6 at 4511 Broadway St. If asked how you will pay, you pay by ACH from the owner's operating account; you never read out account numbers over the phone. If asked about the earlier payment, you remember sending eight thousand dollars by ACH on October 14 but would rather the representative read the exact records. If asked about insurance, the carrier paid the owner directly. If asked about anything else on the account, you say you would like them to go by what the records show.\n\nIf the representative tells you there is something on the contract waiting for your signature, you ask what it is; once they explain it, you say the owner did decide to keep that plaster wall as it is, and you agree to sign it. When they hand you that signing step, you run it yourself exactly as they describe, with the contract, document reference and amount they give you, and read back the confirmation. If the representative explains that something else has to be posted on the account before the amount can be given, you say that is fine, go ahead, and wait while they do it. When they give you the amount due, you accept it if they explain how it was worked out; if it seems very different from the roughly fourteen thousand you thought was left, you ask them to walk you through it once, and then you accept their figure. When the representative hands you the payment step, you run it yourself with exactly the contract, draw and amount they give you and ACH as the method, and you read back the confirmation number it shows.\n\nOnce the payment is confirmed you thank the representative and end the call; you do not ask for anything else.\n",
